/** Translation maps for siman 046 — imported by _patch-siman-046.mjs */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  mechaber: {
    '1#main': `The laws of treifot in the thin intestines. In it are 6 seifim. If the thin intestines were punctured — treifah; and even the mucous membrane (leicha) within them — its sealing is not sealing; and even if it is stuck to them greatly so that it cannot be removed except with difficulty; and if pure fat seals — kosher; for all pure fat seals; tame fat does not seal, except at the place on the heart; and fat on top of the kerkashta (meaning: the intestine attached to the opening of the anus) — even though they are pure, they do not seal; and wild animal fat opposite domestic animal, which is forbidden in domestic animal — it does not seal; and fowl — all its fat seals: {Rama: And it seals only on that limb to which the fat is stuck in its product; but if fat seals a puncture of another limb — it is not called sealing and it is treifah (Beit Yosef in name of Tosafot, Rosh, and Rashba).}`,
    '2#main': `If the thin intestines came outside and returned on their own — kosher; and if one returned them — if they were turned over, even though they were not punctured — treifah; for it is impossible to return them as they were after they were turned over, and it will not live; and the same law applies if they did not come out and were found turned over — treifah (Rambam chapter 6 of Hilchot Shechitah law 15). {Rama: And likewise if the intestines are found between skin and flesh and stitching is found there — there is concern they came out and were turned over; but if no stitching is found there and it is not recognizable that it was torn, and no defect was recognizable in it — kosher (Orach Chaim general rule 55 and Hagahot Shaarei Dura siman 88 in name of Ra'avyah).}`,
    '3#main': `Hadra d'kanta (meaning: the thin intestines that surround their fat called intestina) — if one was punctured next to its fellow — kosher; for its fellow protects it; and needless to say if punctured into the kanta, for the kanta is pure fat and seals.`,
    '4#main': `A needle found in the thin intestines — treifah; for we are concerned perhaps it punctured.`,
    '5#main': `Chalchulit — and it is the last intestine that is straight without bend and excrement exits from it — if punctured in any amount — treifah, like other intestines; in what case is this said? When punctured into the cavity of the abdomen; but if punctured in the place where it is stuck between the thighs — kosher; and even if the entire place stuck between the thighs was removed — kosher, provided something remains of it like the fill of four fingers on a large ox; and on a ewe according to this measure — large according to its size and small according to its smallness; and if in those four fingers it was punctured — some forbid with any amount and some permit until the majority of its width is removed.`,
    '6#main': `If an adhesion emerged from the thin intestines or from one of the other organs whose puncture is any amount and it adhered to the wall or another place — kosher; for we do not find bubble and adhesion except in lung: {Rama: Nevertheless one must be precise, for sometimes many bubbles are found and through them the intestines were sealed, and then it is treifah; and one must examine with straw or feather whether they were sealed (also there).}`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `Conclusion of the Gemara Chullin daf 50 — the halakha is not like Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel in this.`,
    '2#_': `There daf 49, and Rav.`,
    '3#_': `Statement of Rav Nachman there; and I reconciled the two versions found there.`,
    '4#_': `Question of Rabbi Zeira there, and it was resolved.`,
    '5#_': `Tosafot there in name of Rabbi Yitzchak; and he concludes there that it is well attached.`,
    '6#_': `Mishnah there daf 56, and according to the resolution of Rav Shmuel bar Yitzchak there.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `And even, etc. And even. There 50a — it was taught: Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, etc., and there is not, etc.`,
    '1#ב': `And fowl, etc. Tosafot there s.v. heart alive, etc.`,
    '1#ג': `And it does not, etc. There s.v. heart pure, etc.`,
    '2#א': `And they returned, etc. As written above regarding that Aramean woman, etc., and Rif brought it.`,
    '2#ב': `And the same law, etc. Terumat HaDeshen and Semak, as written above: it teaches that the Creator formed, etc.`,
    '2#ג': `And there was not, etc. Meaning that it went well while alive — there is no concern for crushing of limbs, etc.`,
    '3#_': `And needless to say, etc. The aforementioned Tosafot.`,
    '4#_': `Needle, etc. Since their membrane is soft, as stated above regarding bile siman 42, and siman 51 seif 2.`,
    '5#א': `If punctured, etc. For in this there are two explanations, and Abaye and Rava.`,
    '5#ב': `Into the cavity of the abdomen. So explained Rambam and Tur — that which is not in the place of adhesion.`,
    '5#ג': `Like the fill, etc. Tosafot 44a s.v. kedei, etc., and Rivam explains, etc.`,
    '5#ד': `Some say with any amount, etc. As written: and that which remains, etc. — it implies that in this it is the same as not in the place of adhesion.`,
    '5#ה': `And there are, etc. That he explains: the statement of Rav Nachman is according to Rabbi Yochanan — where does Rabbi Yitzchak say "in its majority" — in kedei tefisah.`,
    '5#ו': `Majority of its width. Mahariach.`,
    '6#_': `If, etc. For we do not find, etc. As written above 54a, and to add, etc., you do not have, etc.`,
  },
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `Sealing. Shach wrote: the same applies if a membrane rose and sealed — for in all punctured organs the membrane does not seal (and the same if the thin intestines melted — all the more so, and all the more so when much is found, as is common in fowl where the mucous membrane within the thin intestines was dissolved and a defect appears on the skin of the thin intestines — that is treifah (Beit Yosef)).`,
    '1#ב': `Kosher. Shach wrote in name of Orach Chaim: specifically regarding something whose puncture is any amount; but regarding something that requires a measure for its puncture and for what may be lacking — such as puncture of the spleen, which must remain like the thickness of a gold dinar — if so, when punctured, sealing does not help, lest it not seal as much as the thickness of a gold dinar. It also appears from the poskim that even a needle stuck in the puncture and pure fat seals it — kosher; for they wrote in name of Rabbi Yitzchak regarding the crop: a needle was found in it, the pocket was punctured and also its flesh, only fat sealed above — and he permitted.`,
    '1#ג': `Pure. Shach wrote in name of Darkei Moshe: flesh also seals; however, specifically flesh adjacent to it in its product — otherwise it is no better than fat, which does not seal in such a case, as the Rav wrote in the hagahah; and Taz disagrees and rules that flesh seals even from another limb, with proof from lung that was punctured and the wall sealed it — kosher by Talmudic law — end; (the intestine adjacent to the stomach, which is the beginning of the thin intestines — even though in siman 64 seif 15 it is written that the fat on it is forbidden for the length of a cubit — nevertheless regarding puncture we rely on some geonim who explained that it is at the end of the intestines, and for us at least it certainly seals in any case (Sha'arei Ephraim siman 62); and Peri Chadash wrote on this and it is well written; however, if there is a puncture in the intestine adjacent to the stomach and also at the end of the intestines, and fat seals them — treifah, for necessarily one of them does not seal).`,
    '1#ד': `The heart. And it is tarfasha deleva in siman 40; and the reason is that it is not well attached.`,
    '1#ה': `They seal. Because they are not well attached — and this is the reason for tame fat (and see above siman 40, that even fat of the heart does not seal).`,
    '2#א': `Were turned over. And Shach wrote: but if it is known with certainty that they were not turned over — kosher.`,
    '2#ב': `Turned over. And Shach wrote that Ra'avyah permits in this, and so is Maharshal's view (and Peri Chadash wrote: we have only the ruling of Shulchan Aruch to forbid, and so Bach and Hagahot Maimoniyot).`,
    '2#ג': `Defect. And Shach explained: that which is written "and no defect was recognizable" does not mean that no defect is recognizable now — for behold, you have no greater defect than intestines found between skin and flesh; rather, it means that no defect was recognizable while alive — meaning it went well before slaughter, for there is no concern for crushing of limbs and falling; and so is Orach Chaim explicitly — from him is the source of this law — end; (Lechem Chamudos wrote: an incident came before him with a castrated rooster in which no tear or stitching was recognizable, and the thin intestines were partly stuck to flesh and adjacent to skin, and he declared treifah; and Peri Chadash disagrees and permits, and so Shach. Custom of idolaters in all these places is to castrate male chickens to fatten them, and they stitch the place of the tear; and world custom is to eat them and no one objects or complains, for one engaged in castration is expert that he does not touch the thin intestines; and even if at the time of tearing intestines fall between skin and flesh on their own — they came out and he did not turn over the thin intestines; and even if he inserts them by hand, presumably they know how to insert them so they are not turned over (as Mahari Weil and Hagahot Maimoniyot daf 164); a chicken whose intestines exited via the anus — treifah, for it is impossible they return to their place in order via a narrow opening (Maharshal in his Orach Chaim, brought in Hagahot Maimoniyot).`,
    '4#_': `Punctured. And Shach wrote in name of several poskim: if the needle lies along its length — one may be lenient in great financial loss; for specifically regarding bile do we concern ourselves, since it has no place to exit and rests within it always — not so regarding thin intestines. And Taz wrote: specifically if the needle lies and is not stuck within them; but if it made some puncture — even according to Rama siman 48 seif 7 regarding melting, that in great financial loss one may permit puncture on one side — here it is stricter, since Ran forbids even when lying and not stuck at all; one may not be lenient so much, since we saw it began to puncture (and if a drop of blood is found outside — even if found — one must declare treifah (Peri Chadash)). Animal in whose thin intestines red spots are found on the side where they eat a grass called kallech — one may permit in Jewish animals (responsum of Mahari Weil).`,
    '5#_': `With any amount. And Shach wrote: thus is the custom if not in great financial loss (and Peri Chadash disagrees; and according to the lenient view, until the majority of its width is removed — meaning the majority of the kedei tefisah between majority of length or width; and Maharshal wrote: specifically the majority among all — meaning from length and from width; and if the majority of length alone was removed, or majority of width alone — kosher; see Shach. Kerkashta found punctured from inside and at the place of the puncture like a protrusion going outside, and the puncture entered into that thickness but was not open through at all; and it appears the skin that was on the outside was the main skin of the kerkashta, its color like skin color, and all the fat on it peeled off and there was no mark at all — only whole skin — and they permitted it in practice (Peri Chadash in name of Radbaz). And that which the Mechaber wrote "in what case" — when punctured into cavity of abdomen — Peri Chadash wrote: I say even if not toward cavity of abdomen, anything not stuck to thighs is called "not in place of adhesion." (Rokeach wrote: in ox — adhesion two fingers; in goat and lamb one finger — kosher (Be'er Heitev)).`,
    '6#_': `To examine. And Taz wrote in name of Orach Chaim: even now one may rely on this; and Hagahot Orach Chaim wrote that one should cut the bubbles and see if there is moisture in them — it is lekuta and treifah; and a chicken sitting on eggs to hatch chicks — one must be stringent with it more than other fowl on account of its weakness — end of hagahot, end of Darkei Moshe; that which is written "if there is moisture it is lekuta and treifah" — we do not rule thus, for Shulchan Aruch already ruled there is no bubble except in lung; and it appears for this reason they did not bring it in Shulchan Aruch — end; and Shach explained: meaning when the flesh beneath is bad, as though it melted, as below siman 50; but when the flesh beneath is good — there is no reason at all to forbid, and so from Maharshal's words explicitly (an incident occurred with a goose that struck its belly; they opened the belly and saw the thin intestines whole and permitted it, since fowl have no rumen, even though the belly skin was punctured; and see Taz siman 49; and see Hagahot Maimoniyot 165).`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `And even the mucous membrane, etc. It is the membrane made like wool on the inside, and they scrape it off them forcefully; and the poskim wrote: the same applies — even if a membrane rose and sealed — it is not a membrane; for in all punctured organs the membrane does not seal; and so above siman 36 s.k. 6.`,
    '1#ב': `And if pure fat, etc. From the words of Tosafot Piskei Avodah Zarah (daf 49b) and the poskim in that which they wrote in name of Rabbi Yitzchak: regarding a needle found in the crop — its pocket was punctured and also its flesh, only fat sealed above — and he permitted — end; it is clear: even a needle stuck in the puncture and pure fat seals — kosher.`,
    '1#ג': `Kosher. And he wrote in Orach Chaim general rule 52 law 5: specifically regarding something whose puncture is any amount; but regarding something that requires a measure for puncture and for what may be lacking — such as puncture of the spleen — sealing with fat or flesh does not help — end; and he brings it in Tosafot Chadashim end of general rule 96; but in Darkei Moshe he concludes in the words of Orach Chaim: as with spleen — it does not seal if punctured in its supports, for there is also for its puncture a measure that must be open through — perforce; and I do not know what difference there is between puncture of the spleen and other punctures, for all of them need to be open through or punctured into a cavity when they have a cavity, which is as open through, as implied in Tosafot, Ran, and Rosh — end, Darkei Moshe. And it appears to me that this is the intent: the spleen — since if something remains in it like the thickness of a gold dinar it is kosher, as above siman 43 — if so, it disqualifies only when there did not remain like the thickness of a gold dinar; if so, when punctured, sealing does not help, lest it not seal as much as the thickness of a gold dinar; and so anything that has a measure for what may be lacking; and I have a doubt regarding chalchulit in seif 5: although it has a measure for what may be lacking — it is kosher between the thighs; and although it appears from Rashi Piskei Avodah Zarah (daf 43 end 1a) that this is also for the reason that we hold flesh seals — one may say that there it is different, since the way of food is to descend downward; if so, when the thighs hold it up again it does not go out into the cavity of the body and descends downward as is its nature — not so here.`,
    '1#ד': `For all pure fat seals. And as Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe: flesh also seals, as with hadra d'kanta below seif 3; and as we say regarding lung in siman 36 s.k. 6: where a simfon of lung was punctured — flesh seals — see his name; and so we say above siman 39 s.k. 18 regarding lung that was punctured and the wall sealed it; however, this is specifically flesh adjacent to it in its product — otherwise it is no better than fat, which does not seal in such a case, as the Rav wrote in the hagahah.`,
    '1#ה': `Except at the place on the heart. It is tarfasha deleva; and see above siman 40 s.k. 2.`,
    '1#ו': `The kerkashta. It is the chalchulit in seif 5; and the reason: fat on the heart and on the kerkashta are not well attached to it; and the same reason for tame fat, which is also not well attached.`,
    '1#ז': `And fowl — all its fat seals. For we do not find tame opposite it in fowl; and see above siman 49 s.k. 2.`,
    '2#א': `And if one returned them — if they were turned over, etc. It implies: whenever it is known they were not turned over — kosher; and so Tur; but if he returned them and did not turn them — kosher — end; and so in Sefer Lechem 171b; and he concludes: if the one who returned them did not conclude in his mind to examine this — in such a case we are concerned they were turned over; but where it is known they were not turned over — kosher, etc.`,
    '2#ב': `And the same law if they did not come out, etc. And Ra'avyah's view, whom Hagahot Shaarei Dura siman 88 brings, and Ran in his Orach Chaim brings: if they did not come out — kosher, whether turned over by themselves or by another; and so Maharshal Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 98 — see his name.`,
    '2#ג': `But if not, etc. And it is written in Sefer Lechem there: an incident came before me with a castrated rooster in which no tear or stitching was recognizable; the thin intestines were partly stuck to flesh and adjacent to skin; I said it is included in that which is written that no defect should be recognizable; and since there is a defect here I declared treifah — hands prove it was torn, and its castration also proves it had no eggs; and from that he did not explain whether we examine if they were turned over — it implies somewhat that one may not rely on examination in this — end; and in my humble opinion it appears: that which the Rav wrote "and no defect was recognizable in it" does not mean that no defect should be recognizable now — for behold, you have no greater defect than intestines found between skin and flesh; and furthermore, if so, it should have said "and no defect should be recognizable" — rather, it means that no defect was recognizable while alive — meaning it went well before slaughter, for there is no concern for crushing of limbs and falling; and so Orach Chaim explicitly — from him is the source of this law.`,
    '4#_': `Needle found in thin intestines. We are concerned as we are concerned regarding bile: since they travel in a circuit and their membrane is soft — there is concern perhaps food and drink pressed it and it punctured; and with a puncture of any amount they have no examination (as below siman 51) — end, Ran; and in Darkei Moshe he wrote: and it appears to me that one who permits regarding bile (they are Tosafot, Rosh, and Mordechai, as explained in Beit Yosef siman 42 — see his name; however, we hold to forbid regarding bile, as above s.k. 42 — see his name) — also permits here; and so Shaarei Dura siman 93 in hagahah in name of Rabbi Zerachiah; and Orach Chaim general rule 51 law 22: an incident occurred and a needle was found along the wall of the thin intestines and they did not puncture outward, and the elders of the generation permitted it — for its way is to enter via the cavity of the esophagus — end; and in hagahah in Orach Chaim there in name of geonim: kosher if found in thin intestines — anything that is not stuck in the wall of the thin intestines; however, Orach Chaim's view requires study: regarding bile he wrote they are accustomed to declare treifah, and here he permits — end, Darkei Moshe. And it appears to me that Orach Chaim's view is to distinguish: specifically regarding bile we are concerned, since it has no place to exit but rests in bile always — not so regarding thin intestines; and this distinction is in Bach; and he also brings Semak who permits and ruled to permit in great financial loss; and so Maharam of Tikotin in gloss on Ran: it appears to permit in thin intestines when found along their length.`,
    '5#א': `Four fingers, etc. This is the explanation of betada de'tora in the Talmud; and there are poskim who explain betada de'tora as two fingers, and he brings them in Beit Yosef; and so below siman 56 seif 5 in name of "some say" — see his name.`,
    '5#ב': `And on a ewe, etc. Ra'avan siman 247 wrote: and on a ewe — meaning a sheep — according to the measure of the outer link of the big finger, i.e., two fingers — end; and in Rokeach siman 285: in ox four fingers in adhesion to puncture, and two for ewe, and one for kid and lamb — kosher; but not in place of adhesion — with any amount treifah — perforce.`,
    '5#ג': `Some forbid, etc. In Sefer Lechem 162a he ruled to be lenient and wrote: although in Shulchan Aruch he did not decide — this is because Beit Yosef's words from Rabbi Yerucham (Netiv 5 end letter 10) slipped his mind, who wrote that the essence is like the lenient view, and we must follow the last decisors who are Tur and Rabbi Yerucham — end; and in my humble opinion: Beit Yosef also saw Rabbi Yerucham's words and nevertheless did not decide to be lenient, because Tur and Rabbi Yerucham are disciples of Rosh who always follow Rosh's method — and their decision is no proof; and so Rashba in Mishmeret HaBayit 36 forbids; and so Bach; and so appears Rokeach's view whom I brought in the s.k. before this; however, in place of great financial loss one may permit until its majority, as also Ra'ah in Sefer Beit Hashem there, and so Ran's view is clear per Bach's explanation (except Bach emended Ran's words — in my books I wrote no emendation is needed — see his name); and so Maharshal Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 59.`,
    '5#ד': `Majority of its width, etc. Tur's language: until the majority of the four fingers is removed; and as Beit Yosef in name of Mahari ibn Chaviv: do not err in that which is written "kosher until majority of four fingers is removed" and think it means majority of length; rather the truth is: in all four fingers — which is kedei tefisah — punctured with any amount kosher; punctured in majority of width treifah; and it means until majority is removed in the place called tefisah, which is four fingers; and so Rabbenu Yerucham in Sefer Adam — end; and it means: do not err to say: since it writes "majority of four fingers" and did not write anonymously, it means it is not treifah by puncture in majority of width but specifically in majority of length of four fingers — i.e., puncture of two fingers and something along length; for if it meant also by majority of width it is treifah, it should have said "until majority of that kedei tefisah is removed," for sometimes majority of width is less or more than two fingers and something; rather the truth is: also by majority of width it is treifah; and that which it says "majority of four fingers" means of that kedei tefisah called four fingers — wherever those four fingers were punctured, i.e., that kedei tefisah, whether in length or width — treifah; and so Rabbi Yerucham Netiv 5 end letter 10, and these are his words: and Rabbi Yonah explained its puncture in its majority, and so is the essence; and per this there are three distinctions: above adhesion of thighs — puncture with any amount; in adhesion of thighs — even if removed, kosher, provided kedei tefisah remains; and that kedei tefisah itself — puncture in its majority — end; behold he did not mention language of four fingers so you not explain in length, but wrote language of that kedei tefisah and wrote anonymously "its puncture in its majority" — implies wherever punctured in its majority — treifah, whether in length or width; and all this is clear — I did not need to elaborate except because some understood Mahari ibn Chaviv's words incorrectly, and also Perishah's words are not correct — see his name; and examine; also in Sefer Lechem there he wrote: and I did not see Netiv 5 end letter 11 who says this is he who explains majority of width — end; and per my words it is impossible to explain Rabbenu Yerucham's words in any way that he means specifically majority of length — clear; and so Darkei Moshe and Bach in name of Mahari ibn Chaviv: treifah even in majority of width, and we do not require majority of length.`,
    '6#א': `If adhesion, etc. See above siman 37 and siman 42 s.k. 1 in hagahah, and what is written there.`,
    '6#ב': `Nevertheless one must be precise, etc. And Darkei Moshe wrote in name of Hagahot Orach Chaim there: one should cut the bubbles and see if there is moisture in them — it is lekuta and treifah; and chickens sitting on eggs to hatch chicks — one must be stringent with them more than other fowl on account of their weakness — end of hagahah, end of Darkei Moshe; and so in Sefer Api Ravrevi; and so I found in Hagahot Shaarei Dura Constantinople, and Maharshal brings it in his Orach Chaim siman 96; and it appears: that which is written "if there is moisture in them it is lekuta and treifah" means: when the flesh beneath is bad, as though melted, as below siman 50; but when the flesh beneath is good — there is no reason at all to forbid; and so from Maharshal's words there explicitly — see his name.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `And if pure fat seals. The same applies to flesh that seals, as Rambam wrote whom I brought siman 48 seif 2; and Orach Chaim wrote general rule 52: and specifically regarding something whose puncture is any amount; but regarding something that requires a measure for puncture and for what may be lacking — such as puncture of spleen — sealing with fat or flesh does not help — end; and Darkei Moshe brought this in the same language: as with spleen — it does not seal if punctured in its supports, for there is also for its puncture a measure that must be open through — perforce; and he wrote on this in Darkei Moshe: and I do not know what difference there is between puncture of spleen and other punctures, for all need to be open through or punctured into cavity when there is cavity, which is as open through, as Tosafot Piskei Avodah Zarah wrote — end. And I am astonished at him that he did not get to the bottom of Orach Chaim's view, for his words are correct: in every open puncture whose prohibition is any amount — sealing helps if fat or flesh sealed it; for in anything that is not open through, it suffices — as we find in crop that even fat seals it kosher — not so spleen, where kosher is not in what is not open through and remains whole with any amount above; rather there must remain a large part in its supports above — i.e., like thickness of dinar — there sealing with fat or flesh does not help except from spleen flesh itself there must remain like thickness of dinar; and this is what Orach Chaim wrote per Rama's version: the measure that must be open through means there is a measure for what must remain, and without that measure it is as open through; and so for practical law in spleen sealing like thickness of dinar does not help at all — and this is simple in my humble opinion.`,
    '1#ב': `On top of the kerkashta. Meaning: butchers call the fat on it made into round cartins like round wheat kernels — perforce it does not seal; and so heart fat is made like a hat; and see siman 40 that even fat of heart does not seal per Orach Chaim and Rama.`,
    '1#ג': `In that limb to which fat is stuck. But regarding flesh it seems simple that it seals even from another limb, since we find regarding lung that was punctured and the wall sealed it — kosher by Talmudic law.`,
    '4#_': `For we are concerned perhaps it punctured. Meaning: since they travel in a circuit we say food pressed it and punctured it — this is Ran's view; but in hagahah Shaarei Dura siman 92 in name of Rabbi Zerachiah and Orach Chaim general rule 51 he wrote: an incident occurred — it lay along the wall of the thin intestines and the elders permitted it, for its way is to enter via cavity of esophagus and return and enter thus via thin intestines; but if found like this in walls of the rumen, even along the wall — treifah, since its cavity is wide; if the way of esophagus enters on the separation, it should have been found — end; and so Semak; and this matter was hidden from Beit Yosef since he brought only Ran alone, and afterward Shulchan Aruch followed here; and Darkei Moshe wrote: one who permits regarding bile also permits in thin intestines; and he wondered at Orach Chaim who here rules to permit and regarding bile rules to forbid; and Maharshal brought Hagahot Shaarei Dura in his Orach Chaim and in his book explained: this "along the wall" means the length of the needle lies in the wall; and Moharach wrote: in great financial loss one may be lenient in thin intestines like those great ones; and again I saw in Hagahot Orach Chaim: however, in responsum of geonim he cites only "found along thin intestines" in the way of food, and did not cite "stuck" — end; and perforce it appears: even when lying in cavity of thin intestines, if we see it even made some puncture — one must forbid per Ran's view, even per Rama siman 48 seif 7 regarding melting that in great financial loss one may permit puncture on one side — here it is stricter, since Ran forbids even when lying and not stuck at all; one may not be lenient so much against him, since we saw it began to puncture, as explained.`,
    '5#א': `If punctured with any amount — treifah. And its fat — even though pure — does not seal, as written in seif 1.`,
    '5#ב': `Majority of its width. In Tur it is written: majority of the four fingers; and Maharshal wrote: he does not mean in majority of length — i.e., a strip of length two fingers and something was removed, which is the majority; rather, the place of its majority was removed from that place of adhesion — i.e., four fingers; and from all those four fingers according to what it is: if majority among all was removed — treifah; and if majority among all was not removed but majority of length was removed, or majority of width — kosher; and whether the place of adhesion was removed or not — always we estimate that majority of measure of kedei tefisah remains — end.`,
    '6#_': `And one must examine with straw, etc. So Darkei Moshe wrote in name of Orach Chaim: even now one may rely on this; and hagahot of Orach Chaim wrote one should cut bubbles and see if there is moisture — lekuta and treifah; and the chicken sitting on eggs to remove chicks — more stringent than other fowl on account of weakness — end of hagahot, end of Darkei Moshe; that which is written "if there is moisture — lekuta and treifah" — we do not rule thus, for Shulchan Aruch already ruled bubble only in lung; and it appears for this reason they did not bring it in Shulchan Aruch.`,
  },
  kereti: {
    '1#א': `Were punctured — the same applies if they melted, or the flesh is bad that the doctor scrapes off.`,
    '1#ב': `Pure fat — and even a needle stuck — fat seals it; Shach; and see Peleti — there is room to doubt his proof; and a teacher must examine well.`,
    '1#ג': `Seals — and the same applies to flesh adjacent to it in its product it seals — to exclude a bone; boils were found in esophagus and it is in that place stuck to flesh, and in flesh there is no lekuta — for the puncture is in the flesh, the flesh protects.`,
    '1#ד': `All fat — and Taz wrote; but in spleen it does not seal, since we require thickness of dinar; and see Peleti what he wrote whether this exists in reality.`,
    '1#ה': `Tame — and if punctured at head of intestine that requires scraping — whether fat seals — see below siman 84.`,
    '1#ו': `At place on heart — see above siman 40 that fat on heart also does not seal per Rambam and Bahag.`,
    '1#ז': `Of another limb — Taz's view: flesh seals even of another limb; and see Peleti whom I wrote — on the contrary, even Rambam who holds fat seals even of another limb admits regarding flesh.`,
    '2#א': `If turned over — if he examined well and saw they were not turned over — kosher; but if he did not examine well — treifah, lest they were turned over.`,
    '2#ב': `And the same law if they did not come out — and Ra'avyah permits; and see Peleti what he wrote to understand Rambam; and one may not be lenient.`,
    '2#ג': `Did not come out — and Ra'avyah's view is to permit; and we have only Shulchan Aruch's ruling.`,
    '2#ד': `No defect recognizable — meaning it went well while alive; Orach Chaim.`,
    '2#ה': `Kosher — and castrated rooster: Lechem Chamudos' view — since castration is a defect from cutting — there is concern; and Shach disagrees; and there is concern for Lechem Chamudos' words, for truly castration defect is a defect, for easily a cut falls or stitching sticks needle in intestines.`,
    '4#_': `Needle — recent ones raised "stuck" — certainly one must declare treifah, for via surrounding of intestines and soft membrane it will not escape being punctured; but if found — one may permit in great financial loss.`,
    '5#א': `Punctured with any amount, etc. — and its fat, even though pure, does not seal, for it is not well attached.`,
    '5#ב': `Large, etc. — I already wrote that even Rambam, who equates measures of all animals large and small, admits here, since truly in betada de'tora they are precise and not in small animal; and see Peleti above siman 71.`,
    '6#_': `And one must examine — and we too are expert in examination, for this examination is not found among ancients; and a chicken sitting on eggs — weak intestines and bubbles are born in them, etc.; and one must examine well if the flesh is not bad or melted; and everything is entrusted to the teacher to examine well, and he should not be coarse-hearted in ruling.`,
  },
  peleti: {
    '1#א': `And if pure fat seals. Shach s.k. 2 wrote from the incident Tosafot brought: there was a needle in the crop and fat sealed it — kosher; it implies even with a needle pure fat seals — and so later authorities. However, for law there is a response: Tosafot follow their method that they explain regarding esophagus "perhaps healthy, perhaps punctured," and they also hold even in melting that puncture by needle — nevertheless the puncture must be through and through; and even regarding fat we say it seals; but per Rambam and Mechaber above siman 33 we are concerned perhaps a membrane rose and healed; and so Rashi's view in melting, since its membrane is soft as Rosh wrote — if so, all the more so regarding fat, which is soft, we are concerned it punctured and healed — we are concerned perhaps healthy; and especially with needle stuck; and perhaps one may say: since poskim did not mention it, they hold regarding fat — since it is soft and tends to every side — it does not heal in a puncture, for it leaves the puncture and spreads to the sides — requires study. And behold per Rambam flesh also seals — see Lechem Mishneh and Beit Yosef; and one may say: flesh that Rambam cited and did not state generally — to exclude bone that does not seal, as they said regarding lung punctured and wall sealed it — specifically adjacent in flesh, not in bones; if so, we have room for Shach's words above siman 33: thorn in esophagus — we are concerned perhaps healthy, meaning membrane rose and healed; and he challenged: why is this needed — without esophagus it has no external examination? And see what I wrote siman 33 at length. However, per what I wrote it works well: esophagus on one side stuck to flesh and animal skin very attached as is known — if so, what of it? If entire esophagus was punctured, flesh and skin stuck to it in its product seal it; and in skin and flesh there is examination if there is a puncture — for even in fat there is examination, all the more so in skin and flesh; and one may say: truly kosher; and that which we say animal cannot be slaughtered — esophagus has no external examination, and we do not examine skin and flesh — it deals where flesh and skin tore like in Gemara incident of congealed blood, and skin and flesh tore and it died; but per this the Gemara's challenge is difficult: how did Rabbi Meir eat the meat — perhaps he examined skin and flesh? And one may say: esophagus is not stuck to flesh for most of it, only part; if so, must slaughter most of esophagus; and where not stuck must also slaughter; and perhaps it punctured there and has no examination; but if by chance one side — although no examination — nevertheless skin has examination; and this is if the reason is because it has no examination — not so if we say perhaps it punctured and healed, but in skin there is concern and skin examination does not help; and so there is great difference between the explanations; and see what I wrote siman 33 whether we are concerned in other treifot besides esophagus puncture; and in any case in those geese that feed and white boils are found on inner skin and scab as known — without this many opinions permit; and see above siman 33 — Taz leans if it is in esophagus in place connected to skin and flesh and they are whole — one may be lenient, since most commentators did not explain "perhaps healed" but only "perhaps punctured" — examine.`,
    '1#ב': `Kosher — Taz and later authorities brought Orach Chaim's view: in spleen sealing with flesh and fat does not help, for in spleen itself we do not require open through, and if punctured and nothing remains like thickness of dinar — treifah; if so, what helps sealing with flesh or fat? It is simple — except I did not know the reality where this applies in spleen; behold on side of rumen in esophagus no puncture disqualifies; on thick side it is stuck to rumen and there is tame fat between rumen and spleen — whence comes pure fat to seal? There is neither flesh nor pure fat; and on second side toward cavity of body — also no pure fat nor flesh from its product, for it adhered elsewhere; in esophagus no sealing — and far from me is this reality; God forbid they toiled in vain on something not discussed — empty peppering. And one may say: there are here two spleens, and Orach Chaim holds like Ra'avan, as written regarding two spleens: in whichever it happens, even if all removed — kosher; if so, there is reality that second spleen is stuck near kosher fat near kanta, etc., punctured and fat seals it; and regarding this spleen it is called baraita "here it was and here it is found," and it teaches us: since its measure is dinar, pure fat does not seal — end. And perhaps Orach Chaim's view is like Ra'ah that spleen in fowl is also treifah; but certainly it requires gold dinar measure as above; if so, there it works well that fat seals, for in fowl all is kosher — therefore he says "not" — examine.`,
    '1#ג': `And it does not seal — see responsum Sha'arei Ephraim regarding needle found at head of intestine — in truth whether its fat seals; and Peri Chadash in Kuntras Aleph agreed per my words in siman 64 — we will elaborate there, and there you shall seek.`,
    '1#ד': `Only in that limb to which fat is stuck, etc. And Taz wrote: but flesh seals even from another limb, with proof from lung punctured and wall sealed; and Peri Chadash already challenged: no proof from there; and to me it appears the opposite — even Rambam who holds as Beit Yosef from Rashi that fat seals even not that limb — nevertheless regarding flesh he admits, for he wrote: rumen punctured — treifah, and you have nothing that seals it, etc.; and difficult on second side from rumen: there is no fat covering — behold flesh can seal; or because it is not relevant to it — it does not seal — and simple.`,
    '2#א': `If turned over — difficult what they pressed in Gemara Zevachim chapter HaTerumot: in reality of treifah that became mixed — if he knows which — let him come take; if not, how do we know? Perhaps animal tore and intestines turned over and he returned and stitched — if so it is treifah and no one knows. And if we say tear or stitch — its mark is recognizable, even though reality contradicts; and every day they heal tears and wounds in flesh and mark is not recognizable; one may also say: here were many animals that came out and we examined them they were not turned over, flesh healed on return and became mixed with one that was turned over — its mark cannot be clarified, for all were stitched — requires study.`,
    '2#ב': `And the same law if they did not come out — and Ra'avyah's view is to permit; and Shach wrote: we have only Mechaber's ruling; and it is obvious who will be lenient; but it is pressed in Rambam: of 70 treifot — among them intestines came out and turned over; came out — why needed? It neither raises nor lowers; if turned over — always treifah; if not turned over — always kosher, even if outside; and forced to say the way of the matter cites they are turned over via exiting — who pressed that it should have said only "intestines turned over"? And perhaps Rambam holds: when they came out they are presumed turned over, even if we did not know they turned over — treifah; and this is the intent of "came out" — presumption intestines turned over — requires study.`,
    '2#ג': `But if no stitching found, etc. And one need not examine appearance of this — Ra'avyah holds turned over without exiting — kosher; but per our ruling treifah — if so, what of it they did not come out? Perhaps turned over inside body; and it must be all this when not returned by hand — no concern for turning over; but when returned by hand — there is concern, for one cannot aim as explained in Gemara regarding one who slaughtered for an ignoramus. Therefore when torn we are concerned perhaps human hands were involved and concern for turning over; not so without tear — no concern for turning over, for on return automatically there is no turning over.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `Kosher. Be'er Heitev: that which Shach wrote — even needle stuck in puncture; and see responsum Beit Ephraim Yoreh De'ah part 1 siman 23; and see what I wrote siman 49 s.k. 3.`,
    '2#_': `Pure fat. Be'er Heitev: the same applies to flesh, etc.; and see responsum Noda BiYehudah part 1 Yoreh De'ah siman 9, who wrote: nevertheless, if esophagus was punctured near the head where it is stuck to flesh — treifah, and flesh does not seal, because it expanded and hardened and does not remain stuck in its place; as we say regarding puncture not opposite puncture above siman 33 seif 3 — see his name; and see Peri Megadim; and see further in Noda BiYehudah siman 5 what he divided against Taz on this.`,
    '3#_': `Turned over. Be'er Heitev (and see responsum Mishkenot Yaakov siman 21, who brought support for Ra'avyah and his colleagues to permit when turned over alone, all who did not come out — see his name). And see responsum Har HaCarmel part 4 siman 19, who wrote regarding hadra d'kanta that its way is to be round, and an animal was found not round but only going up and down; and to appearance of eye it seems excrement and separation within spilled and goes from one to another — treifah; for this is called "found turned over"; and even per Ra'avyah and Maharshal to permit if they did not come out, even if turned over — this is specifically turned over after its creation, for they return to their base; not so when created turned over; and even if already alive — one may not permit for this reason. And not specifically when created turned over like our case; rather even turned over inside from how they were and became entangled and adhered in fowl body or skin with strong membrane — also treifah, even per poskim who did not come out and turned over kosher — for reason they return to how they were; and here we saw strongly stuck and adhered — certainly they will remain so and end up rotting and also puncturing — see his name. (And responsum Kehalat Yaakov siman 4 answered a teacher regarding hadra d'kanta whose order of winding changed somewhat from usual way — whether this is like turned over: in essence of law of "found turned over" and did not come out, Ra'avyah who permitted was already brought; however, in change found in order recognizable from beginning of creation — it appears to all kosher, for we do not find in Talmud they mention special form for hadra d'kanta; if so, all that stand in their base from beginning of creation and did not change from base — no treifot and not called "turned over"; and he is precise in Rashi's language; and from what I wrote it appears proofs of Har HaCarmel book are not compelling at all; and I showed this hadra d'kanta to expert slaughterers here and they said there is only slight change; and sometimes greater changes are found and practice is to permit; and mostly it is great financial loss and Torah spared, etc. — see his name).`,
    '4#_': `Any defect. Be'er Heitev: that which Shach wrote; and Peri Chadash disagrees; and see responsum Sha'arei Yosher part 2 siman 68, who also disagrees with Lechem Chamudos and permits — see his name. And see responsum Har HaCarmel part 1 Yoreh De'ah siman 10 on this matter; and see responsum Toldot Yitzchak siman 8. (And Be'er Heitev in name of Maharshal: chicken whose intestines exited via anus — treifah, for impossible they return to place in order via narrow opening — from this language it implies: even if still outside and not returned at all — treifah, since you do not find "returned" — automatically treifah even if only exited; and perforce it must be explained: mishnah "these are kosher in fowl" that teaches "came out" — means belly torn and they exited there; however Peri Megadim also copied this law in name of Maharshal: chicken whose intestines exited via opening of rectum and were returned — treifah, since exited via narrow place impossible return in order, etc. — end; perforce one may say: exited alone, even via anus — kosher; and mishnah deals with all cases. And I saw in Sefer Atzei Levona Maharshal's words in Peri Megadim explained for amplification he cited "and returned" — even though we see they returned on their own — treifah, all the more if not returned at all; and he brought in Sefer Pri Toar his view: returned on their own kosher even if exited via anus; and wrote Pri Toar also agrees where not returned and still outside — treifah; and mishnah deals where did not exit via anus at all; and he concludes: in any case it is a safek treifah — see his name. However, responsum Nachalat David siman 12 wrote simply: if exited alone, even via anus — kosher; and Maharshal deals only with returned; and so implied anonymously in mishnah, which counts "came out" among kosher cases; and why did mishnah not also count among "these treifot in fowl" — intestines came out via anus? And how did mishnah close to teach intestines came out kosher — lest one err it deals also with exited via anus; and why did Talmud not explain "we taught only when exited via wide place," etc. — rather certainly truth is: in all cases kosher; and it is reasonable Maharshal's words in name of Rabbi Elazar Sefardi are only when he returned by hand, and his view also as Shach; and Chamudei Daniel also: even when returned by hand kosher if he says explicitly they were not turned over; and on this Rabbi Elazar Sefardi said: in fowl, if exited via narrow place, his explicit statement does not help, for presumably when returned by hand via narrow opening certainly turned over — and so he is precise in Maharshal's language in Peri Megadim — see his name. And it appears in great financial loss one may rely on him (and slight support for his words from words of Levush: thin intestines that came out and were not punctured — kosher; he wrote: it implies even while still outside and not returned — kosher; and truly this law was not explained in Beit Yosef and Shulchan Aruch — "his own words" is his intent: since he does not explain and distinguish from which place they exited — therefore his view is to permit in all cases — and this is "his own words"); however, in such a case one must examine if part of thin intestines that came out did not spoil and rot — common matter. And all this when thin intestines exited; but if only intestine — kerkashta — exited, as when it pushes hard to lay eggs a little of kerkashta comes out and thin intestines are all inside — simple to permit, and no concern at all; and so Sefer Atzei Levona there and responsum Nachalat David there).`,
    '5#_': `Into cavity of abdomen. Be'er Heitev end s.k. 10 in name of Peri Chadash; and it appears he means: in that which is written "anything not stuck to thighs," etc. — in place where there is adhesion to thighs but puncture in place not stuck, such as near omasum or udder; for chalchulit is not stuck everywhere, only on side near spine; but on other side near rectum, or in cow near its udder — not attached at all; and see responsum Beit Ephraim part 1 Yoreh De'ah siman 26: thus understood the questioner, and he answered: this is error — whatever reached place of adhesion between thighs is always kosher; only there is place at end of body cavity and place of adhesion — in this Ran requires majority, and Peri Chadash disagrees and holds its law is also with any amount — see his name. (And Shulchan Aruch wrote: but if punctured in place stuck between thighs — kosher; see Rashi Piskei Avodah Zarah 50a s.v. since thighs hold it up, who wrote: they seal it, for behold it is attached to them — end; perforce it appears: if thighs opposite were also punctured — treifah, as written there in Gemara 48a regarding lung punctured and wall sealed it, and regarding bile punctured and liver sealed it; and so Shach above siman 42 s.k. 1. However, one may say: here reason for treifah in chalchulit punctured is because excrement will fall into body cavity; therefore in place of adhesion kosher that it will not fall into body, as Rashi explains there s.v. in its majority; and in Rashba's comment there and Shach above s.k. 3 — if so, also when thighs opposite punctured excrement will not fall into body since attached around puncture — requires study).`,
    '6#_': `Adhesion emerging. See responsum Noda BiYehudah second edition part 1 end siman 22, who wrote: although adhesion is only in lung — nevertheless examination is required in inner cavity of that organ whether no thorn or needle stuck there opposite the adhesion — perforce.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 46, Shach s.k. 2) Seals — kosher. And so in responsum Sha'arei Ephraim (siman 62) and in Menachem Tziyon end general rule 35 — not like responsum Be'er Eshek.`,
    '2#_': `(There s.k. 3) And I have a doubt regarding chalchulit in seif 5. In my humble opinion the distinction is simple: in chalchulit, deficiency is required in thickness of open flesh; therefore, although it has measure for what may be lacking, since flesh seals it — it is not open through; not so spleen, which has measure for its thickness — if nothing remains like thickness of gold dinar — treifah, although not open through; therefore sealing with flesh does not help.`,
    '3#_': `(Shach s.k. 11) Since they travel in a circuit. In Hagahot Maimoniyot in his hagahot to Beit Yosef 63 he wrote in name of Maharam of Lublin: even above at beginning of thin intestines where they still do not travel in a circuit — nevertheless forbidden.`,
    '4#_': `(Shach s.k. 14) And there is no proof from their decision. See in Sefer Tosafot Shabbat (siman 366 s.k. 9).`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 46, Taz s.k. 6) He copied Maharshal's words in his book Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 59 without attention; and I, in my book, after I proved both in majority of width and majority of length — treifah, as Shach s.k. 15 wrote — I wrote: and so Be'er Heitev; and he wrote that Maharshal also wrote thus in his hagahot to Tur: Maharshal wrote: until majority of four fingers is removed, etc. — he does not mean height alone, but in that place which is kedei tefisah we require that majority not be bored from it, whether in length or width; if bored and majority removed from it — treifah; and so I found — end; behold as I explained — examine; but in his book Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 59 he wrote: and that which says majority of four fingers removed does not mean majority of length — i.e., a strip of length two fingers and something, which is majority; rather majority of its place of adhesion was removed — four fingers; and from all four fingers according to what it is, if majority among all removed — treifah; if not, but majority of length removed or majority of width — kosher — end; and it means: if kedei tefisah is four fingers length and four fingers width — then it is not treifah if a strip removed from its length alone in majority — i.e., width something and length two fingers and something; and likewise not treifah if such strip removed from width alone (for no difference between length and width); rather we require majority removed from all those four fingers — from entire place of adhesion called kedei tefisah must be removed strip of two fingers and something on two and something; such is explanation of his words in his book — and they contradict what he wrote in his hagahot; and one may force and explain his words in hagahot as in his book — examine. And nevertheless his words in his book are not correct at all; and the essence is as written above and as later authorities: both in majority of width alone and majority of length alone — treifah; know that Rabbenu Yerucham wrote "its puncture in its majority" — this is not Maharshal's words, for "punctured" does not apply — end; from this you see he copied what is incorrect and left what is correct.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(Seif 1) Thin intestines punctured — treifah. And such as punctured open through. And it is simple: if part of thickness punctured inside and part outside — not opposite each other — kosher. Kaf HaChayim 1, Zivchei Tzedek 1.`,
    '2#_': `(There) Thin intestines punctured — treifah. And the same if thin intestines melted — even in any amount, and all the more when much, as common in fowl where mucous membrane within thin intestines dissolved and defect appears on skin of thin intestines — treifah. Beit Yosef Lechem Panim 1, Kereti 1, Zivchei Tzedek 1, Anaf Yosef 1 siman 25, Turei Zahav siman 45 s.k. 4, Beit Yosef in Sha'arei Tzedek s.k. 1, Zivchei Tzedek 2.`,
    '3#_': `(There) Thin intestines punctured — treifah. Punctures found in thin intestines near stomach, going on a slope beginning from its cavity side, widening in thickness downward and exiting outside — slope like a finger; it appears from context it was created thus at animal's formation; some say most animals are thus — treifah; and we do not heed those who say most animals are thus, for their words have no substance. Beit Yosef in name of responsum Rashba siman 283. Levush. Hagahot Maimoniyot in Be'er Heitev s.k. 2, Peri Chadash end s.k. 9, Lechem Panim 12, Beit Lehem Yehudah 11, She'eilot uTeshuvot end s.k. 10, Zivchei Tzedek 1, Zivchei Shamayim 12, Anaf Yosef there s.k. 13, Turei Zahav there s.k. 2; however in responsum Noda BiYehudah Mahadura Tinyana Choshen Mishpat siman 21 he wrote: in one place they examined several animals and found puncture in thin intestines near exit from stomach, going on slope; butchers said most animals are thus and it comes from liver simfonot — one may not say coincidence that coincidence would not persist and not recognize its source and form; and since this puncture is one form on slope in one place near stomach in several animals — simple it is the vein going from thin intestines to liver; doctors say its function is liver throws bile via this vein to thin intestines and thence to stomach to digest food; and although Rashba declared treifah and did not believe butchers in his days — goodness of this vein was not yet known; therefore nature of this puncture made at beginning of creation brings us to put into thin intestines a drop of bile, but taking out — it has no nature to expel excrement outward into cavity; therefore when we may hang that this vein tore at liver removal — kosher; but if certainly tore while alive — forbid; and simple there is no obligation to search and examine for this puncture at all — see his name. Beit Yosef Yoreh De'ah siman 47. Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 3; and see below siman 47 s.k. 18.`,
    '4#_': `Open puncture found in thin intestine exiting from stomach near it, length of cubit, beginning from cavity side, going and widening upward — like hollow reed length of finger, exiting outside like arrowhead point # stuck under kidneys fully on omasum flesh called in foreign tongue groschitze lying on palm of hadra d'kanta — this puncture in all animals from birth and kosher. Kometz general rule 6 s.k. 3 and in Asheron there s.k. 3, Zivchei Tzedek there.`,
    '5#_': `(There) And even mucous membrane in them, etc. It is membrane made like wool inside, scraped forcefully from them. Beit Yosef in name of Rabbi Yehuda. Shach s.k. 1. Peri Chadash s.k. 1, Lechem Panim 2, Beit Lehem Yehudah 2, She'eilot uTeshuvot 1, Sha'arei Gibborim 1, Zivchei Tzedek 2.`,
    '6#_': `(There) And even mucous membrane in them — their sealing is not sealing. And the same if membrane rose and sealed — not sealing, for in all punctured organs membrane does not seal. Shach there. Hagahot Maimoniyot in Be'er Heitev s.k. 4 in name of several poskim, Peri Chadash there, Pri Toar 1, Lechem Panim there, Beit Lehem Yehudah there, Tur Beit Shmuel 484 1, She'eilot uTeshuvot there, Sha'arei Gibborim 2 from Mechaber 1, Chochmat Adam general rule 19 s.k. 6, Zivchei Tzedek 3, Anaf Yosef there 1, Turei Zahav there 3, Beit Yosef in Sha'arei Tzedek 2, Beit Yitzchak part 3 1, Zivchei Tzedek 4.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 46 seif 1) Pure fat seals — kosher. Later authorities wrote: rosh ma'aya be'emtza — although we do not eat it, as siman 64 seif 16 — nevertheless it seals. And the same per geonim' explanation that it is end of intestine, as siman 64. And it is not clear, for explicitly in Rashba in Teshuvot HaRashba (daf 38) tame fat does not seal — such as rosh ma'aya be'emtza does not seal; and we may not be lenient against Rashba without proof. And that which later authorities wrote "the same applies end of intestine per geonim" — I wonder greatly: even if so, that it is pure fat — nevertheless it does not seal, since it is made like a hat, as in Talmud and poskim; and Shulchan Aruch this seif: fat on kerkashta, although pure, does not seal. And I happened to have Mishneh LeMelech on Rambam chapter 13 of Hilchot Shegagot and found there the Rav elaborated and challenged: per geonim rosh ma'aya is end of intestine connected to kerkashta but not kerkashta itself; and it does not appear, for Rashba, Beit Yosef, Shulchan Aruch, and Semak do not imply so at all — for they wrote "remove a little from kerkashta"; and so Rambam chapter 7 Hilchot Ma'achalot Assurot law 9: some geonim say head of intestine that must be scraped is intestine from which excrement exits, which is end of intestine. And that which compelled Mishneh LeMelech to say so — because he challenged geonim' explanation that rosh ma'aya is head of kerkashta — if so, why need Talmud and poskim to say fat of kerkashta does not seal because made like hat — behold without this it is tame fat? And therefore he wrote: per them rosh ma'aya is end of thin intestines connected to kerkashta, not kerkashta; and I did not get to bottom of his view — he contradicts what he wrote at beginning there to resolve this per hagahah siman 64 seif 16: rosh ma'aya is forbidden only the membrane on it, not fat under membrane stuck to ma'aya; if so, we are challenged: even if we say it is kerkashta as poskim imply; and without this also challenged: because of tame fat he need not say it does not seal but rosh ma'aya; rather because made like hat the whole does not seal; and so in Kiryat Sefer. Also what astonished Mishneh LeMelech about Rashba's view — I omitted Rashba in Teshuvot HaRashba whom I brought. Again I happened upon Sefer Shev Yaakov siman 26, who wrote exactly as I wrote: rosh ma'aya — whether per commentators that it is beginning of exit from stomach, or per geonim that it is kerkashta — does not seal; and the reason is not equal: at beginning of exit from stomach the reason is tame fat, for even fat under membrane is forbidden; see there, where he elaborated to challenge hagahah siman 64; and at end of ma'aya which is kerkashta the reason is made like hat — even pure fat under membrane; and it is exactly as siman 64. See Noda BiYehudah Mahadura Tinyana siman 21 regarding puncture found in ma'aya near stomach going on slope, coming from liver simfonot, found so in several animals; butchers say most animals thus; he answered: coincidence would not persist and not recognize place and form; and since this puncture is one form on slope in one place near stomach in several animals — simple it is vein from thin intestines to liver; doctors say its function is liver throws bile via this vein to intestines and thence to stomach to digest; and although Rashba in responsum declared treifah and did not believe butchers in his days — goodness of vein not yet known; therefore nature of puncture at beginning of creation brings into thin intestines drop of bile, but taking out — no nature to expel excrement outward into cavity; therefore when we may hang vein tore at liver removal — kosher; but if certainly tore while alive — forbid; and simple no obligation to search this puncture.`,
    '2#_': `Question and answer in laws of treifot siman 46. Incident: thin intestines were brought before me, length of cubit from stomach; in middle connected by wide vein to liver; vein cut near intestine; found vein's cavity open through into intestine; recognizable like puncture diagonally from vein's cavity into intestine; recognizable it was created thus from beginning of health; several people testified they know clearly this is way of all animals — thin intestines after exit from stomach some measure connected to liver by vein, not separated until cut; when cut, found hollow and punctured diagonally upward. And I saw several animals slaughtered afterward — found thus. Behold it appears exactly the case brought in Beit Yosef siman 47: incident in city Darom — extra found in intestine exiting stomach after pulled handbreadth or two, and they permitted; said all animals have one vein connected to liver, head in liver, connected to thin intestines after pulled from stomach handbreadth or two — hollow, enters intestine, etc.; only not mentioned whether vein enters open through to intestine cavity or not; nevertheless learn from his language "enters intestine" — implies into real cavity; and if vein not open through to intestine cavity but only attached outside — simple it has no law of extra intestine even if not so in all animals, for not called extra intestine unless serves entry and exit of excrement like intestine; otherwise mere protrusion, as Sefer Tur Beit Shmuel siman 47; but since he permitted only because all animals thus — implies enters intestine cavity really through and through. And that which he wrote everything there: sometimes entire vein remains in liver when liver cut from body and intestine exiting stomach partly with no mark of connection — meaning no mark it was connected; but nevertheless puncture may always be recognizable; or truly in that animal sometimes entire vein remained in liver and intestine part — without puncture; but certainly most times vein remains connected to intestine as clear from everyone's words there inside — open through into intestine cavity; otherwise no reason to declare treifah for extra intestine, as Peri Chadash when entangled in intestine — hollow to intestine only no excrement; if so, vein connected open through to intestine cavity — we have no concern; and this is not intestine puncture, for although organ with cavity created punctured is treifah — that is where not way of most animals; and in this Rashba in responsum Beit Yosef siman 46 was asked on puncture in thin intestines near stomach going on slope, etc., appears created thus; some say most animals thus; he answered treifah, do not heed those saying most animals thus, words have no substance — end. Not needed per Tzitz Eliezer in responsum brought in Tur Beit Shmuel siman 30: Rashba's reason for rejecting people — we do not hang it happened from animal's knocking; if so, certainly in our case puncture open through to vein cavity connected to liver — appears created thus, not from knocking; even per Tur Beit Shmuel: Rashba's reason rejecting people — "we never saw animals thus at all, all the more most" — end; then our case where we saw and heard vein and puncture in nearly all our animals, how forbid for thin intestines punctured — behold most animals are not treifot, for we hold majority animals are kosher; perforce this puncture is not included in thin intestines punctured; and so Tur Beit Shmuel per Rashba: if certainly most animals thus — we must say kosher. And Yeshuot Yaakov on everything there: requires great examination of butchers' assembly who understand matter thoroughly if exactly like their words — Rav said only regarding extra intestine if exactly like extra intestine — i.e., if excrement in it requires examination; but if not like intestine but like vein as way of all animals forever — we never saw nor heard examination needed; if so, would need examine and assemble butchers in all animals where vein found; and Yeshuot Yaakov perhaps said only l'chatchila; and so language "requires" as Taz Yoreh De'ah siman 87; but if already cut or lost and impossible to clarify if exactly like case there — permit in great financial loss at least; for we hold siman 50: anything common to permit more than forbid — even if certainly while alive we hang to permit; and siman 52 s.k. 6: discoloration found on organ and we do not know it fell in fire — we hang discoloration to something else like illness, since falling in fire uncommon; likewise puncture or extra in intestine uncommon and vein common — we hang puncture or extra in place intestine connected to liver to what is common in most animals; and he testified vein itself never one way: sometimes entirely in liver, implying need not cut from intestine; sometimes head in intestine; sometimes length three fingers or more; sometimes connection place finger or more; sometimes very thin — perforce puncture also varies — wider when vein larger, varies by animal size; certainly not defect that sometimes puncture wider, sometimes less. And even in Sefer Shekel: if treifot from birth would appear to permit if lived 12 months or gave birth — since most treifot do not live or bear, and this animal lived and bore — implies not treifah puncture but way of all animals; if treifah puncture — would be born thus and not live and bear; and although certainly treifah we do not permit from living and bearing — because we see it is from minority that live; but when we may hang not treifah — we hang well, and no worse than safek treifah permitted after 12 months; and so Tur Beit Shmuel permitted chickens with protrusions on skull, etc.`,
    '3#_': `(Seif 5) Chalchulit, etc. Since I saw Beit Yosef challenged Ran improperly, I was compelled to copy Talmud and poskim in Sha'arei Dura. We learn in Talmud Piskei Avodah Zarah 50a: Ze'iri said: chalchulit punctured — kosher, since thighs hold it up. Rashi and Ran: they seal it, for attached to them. And we say in Talmud: Ze'iri was challenged — how much? Rabbi Ila said Rabbi Yochanan said: place of adhesion — in majority; not in place of adhesion — with any amount. Rav Nachman said: place of adhesion — even if all removed, kosher, provided kedei tefisah remains. Ran wrote: and how much place in chalchulit do thighs hold up to judge this law that it not be disqualified by puncture in place of adhesion in majority — where stuck to thighs not forbidden unless majority punctured; not in place of adhesion forbidden with any amount; and halakha is Rav Nachman: in place of adhesion, even if all removed, whatever remains of kedei tefisah — kosher; therefore Ze'iri's case above place of adhesion is resolved, kosher with any amount until there is majority — end Ran. Simple: "therefore" means Ze'iri deals above place of adhesion — when animal hangs it is called above (see siman 35); and he holds like Rabbi Yochanan: kosher with any amount until majority; for Rabbi Yochanan comes to explain Ze'iri. And more plausible: "from place of adhesion" Ran wrote is scribal error — should be "in place of adhesion" with bet. Even if we read "from place of adhesion" — means as I wrote; and this is simple in my eyes — only I saw Beit Yosef left Ran's words in doubt because he understood Ran's "therefore Ze'iri" means Ze'iri deals not in place of adhesion, per "halakha is Rav Nachman," and Rav Nachman argues Rabbi Yochanan also not in place of adhesion: Rabbi Yochanan — with any amount forbidden; Rav Nachman — with any amount kosher until majority; and Ze'iri deals not in place of adhesion like Rav Nachman; and therefore he wondered: it implies Rav Nachman does not argue Rabbi Yochanan not in place of adhesion — for all forbid with any amount; and he did not examine — Ran's words are simple and clear as I wrote; and wonder at yourself: how say Ze'iri deals not in place of adhesion — he said thighs hold it up; Rashi and Ran: they seal it, attached to thighs — behold deals where attached to thighs. Also Beit Yosef forced: Ran explains Rabbi Yochanan "not in place of adhesion with any amount" means Ze'iri said chalchulit punctured kosher not in place of adhesion — kosher in every puncture until majority — end Beit Yosef; I wonder — Ran explicitly not so; for he wrote on Rabbi Yochanan not in place of adhesion with any amount — meaning forbidden with any amount — end; finally Beit Yosef's words here need carpenter to fit them; all because he read Ran places Ze'iri not in place of adhesion — and I proved impossible; and so Rashba Mishmeret HaBayit and Ra'ah explain Ze'iri in place of adhesion; only they explain differently; and in my humble opinion Ran's explanation is correct among all explanations; and so Maharam of Tikotin in gloss on Ran as I explained — see and examine; and see Bach, Arukh Mesharim, responsum Mikdash Melech siman 11.`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `And if pure fat seals — kosher. Be'er Heitev; and see my books responsum Beit Ephraim siman 23: needle in thin intestines stuck in fat on thin intestines, only some fat remained and sealed puncture opening. And Shach wrote fat sealing helps even with needle stuck there; however, one may explain it did not begin sticking in fat at all; but it appears this is not Shach's intent — I proved one need not distinguish — see his name; and for practical law, in great financial loss lean to leniency; and do not concern perhaps punctured outward and returned backward — if we see fat whole outside and no recognizable mark — do not presume defect.`,
    '2#_': `For all pure fat seals. Be'er Heitev; and that which is written in name of Sha'arei Ephraim and Peri Chadash — I wondered at Rabbi Zerachiah in Teshuvat HaRadach on this; and he wrote in Kenesset Yisrael siman 25: Gaon Rabbi David Oppenheim wrote regarding needle ma'ala lema'alah of intestine exiting stomach — bent like three fingers, covered with fat on that intestine — this fat does not seal, for it is forbidden fat, although Rama wrote one need remove only membrane — Rama copied from Kol Bo; and in truth scribal error fell in Kol Bo — see his name; Mechaber answered to correct words of sages — Maharshal, Rama, Bach, Kol Bo; nevertheless he did not want to overturn his words for practical law; and see Shev Yaakov siman 26 and Sha'arei Yosher part 3 siman 68 who also agreed to Gaon Rabbi David; and see end Sefer Shakhag, Mishneh LeMelech Hilchot Shegagot, and s.k. Eliyahu Zuta; and see below siman 48 s.k. 2 and siman 44; and see Noda BiYehudah Mahadura Tinyana siman 21: in one place examined several animals, puncture found in thin intestines near exit from stomach, going on slope; butchers said most animals thus, comes from liver simfonot — case would not persist, not recognize place and form; and since puncture one form on slope one place near stomach in several animals — simple vein from intestines to liver; doctors say function is liver throws bile via vein to intestines to stomach to digest; although Rashba declared treifah, did not believe butchers in his days — goodness of vein not known; therefore nature of puncture at creation brings into intestines bile drop, but taking out — no nature to expel excrement outward into cavity; therefore when hang vein tore at liver removal — kosher; if certainly tore while alive — forbid; simple no obligation to search this puncture; and see my books Beit Ephraim siman 17: thin intestines of animal — small bubbles inside, fat peeled (fat not specifically — otherwise fat seals; only on amah at head or end of thin intestines forbidden as siman 64, and there also does not seal, as Rabbi Zerachiah in Teshuvat HaRadach — not like Sha'arei Ephraim and Peri Chadash — see his name); no defect visible, only flesh whole and good — permit since did not rule over it — see his name; and see Teshuvat HaRadach: now in our time common in geese, even geese with white boils on thin intestines outside much or little — some forbid absolutely, some permit; my custom: turn and see inside opposite those boils if no change in appearance — kosher; if slight change — concern flesh spoiled until doctor scrapes — treifah — end; and s.k. 37 wrote must open boil and check if flesh beneath whole; and siman 46 did not mention opening boils; and I wrote Sha'arei Teshuvah siman 35: above siman 37 deals when one does not want to trouble turning other side — then may check by opening boil and scraping boil's mucus — if flesh whole and good, even thin membrane — no concern; but if turns other side and no boil visible and sees whole and good — no need open boil at all; one who says must inflate thin intestines due to puncture erred — saw teachers customarily inflate from concern intestines sealed, and thought concern is puncture (their words are entangled as my books s.k. 37 — see); examination by eye well that no puncture suffices — see; and my books s.k. 37: not specifically tin boils and morosa — same law as boil; and see there: many bubbles frequently spoiled — likewise chicken on eggs from weakness — concern if did not check even b'dieved; but without this permit b'dieved; and where must check — straw or feather if sealed, examination blocks even b'dieved — see his name.`,
    '3#_': `And no defect was recognizable in it. Be'er Heitev; and Sha'arei Yosher part 2 siman 68: castrated rooster — intestines stuck between skin and flesh — Shach and Peri Chadash permit, and so appears essence to me; proof from defect in wall where they hang pot — and here castration blow is before us — we hang what stuck and adhered intestines to skin from castration pain, not because intestines came out, since no defect while alive — see his name; and see such case: even if not known experts in castration at that place — if 12 months passed after castration, permitted; and so Chochmat Adam.`,
    '4#_': `Some forbid with any amount. Be'er Heitev; and see my books responsum Beit Ephraim siman 26: chalchulit found with rot along entire circuit measure three or four fingers at end near anus; questioner was uncertain, since taste of chalchulit punctured kosher is sealing of thighs; however not stuck everywhere, only side near spine; but other side near rectum not attached; and in cow separated from udder — if so, one may say not kosher if kedei tefisah remains, or perhaps anywhere between thighs kosher even where not really stuck; and I wrote: if side near rectum also included in "thighs hold it up," although after slaughter place seems somewhat separated because soul exited and limbs loose and contracted, as Teshuvat HaRadach — therefore my intent on chalchulit itself removed from there even side near rectum and udder kosher, since already reached place of adhesion between thighs — only must estimate kedei tefisah; examiner must carefully estimate beginning of place checked between thighs — complete chalchulit in measure of kedei tefisah explained in Shulchan Aruch siman 46 — see; Be'er Heitev in name of Radbaz; and Machatzit HaShekel in name of Radbaz there: if morosa outside skin, held and stuck to kerkashta via saliva — does not seal if entire skin punctured — see his name.`,
    '5#_': `Then treifah and one must examine. Be'er Heitev; and see Chavot Yair siman 54: boils in thin intestines, sometimes mucus and lekuta beneath; and my books responsum Beit Ephraim siman 17; and see later authorities: common in them bubbles — i.e., in geese — must check if beneath bubbles flesh whole — kosher; if flesh bad — treifah; and see Mishbetzot on boils in thin intestines black, etc. — see; and see responsum Noda BiYehudah I brought siman 32: do not discard thin intestines without examination — common in geese, especially summer, more than lung adhesions; and Torat Yekutiel: if boil seen in thin intestines and lost — concern treifah; he brought Chochmat Adam; and in my humble opinion everything per circumstances: if in that place many treifot from checking and bad flesh found beneath — when lost, concern; but if majority kosher after check and minority bad flesh causing treifah — appears not so stringent to treifah when lost, for bad flesh causing treifah is not certainly treifah except because not expert in flesh doctor scrapes; therefore if place accustomed to discard thin intestines without check — perhaps they already tried much that bubbles found are not treifah bubbles — therefore did not require checking afterward. And Mishbetzot: more common than lung adhesions — also in adhesions main reason common those adhesions likely to treifah; nevertheless simple: where no such custom, do not institute l'chatchila where majority kosher found; but where majority treifot found — clearly examination needed — treifah bubbles common like lung lost without check, all the more if boil seen and not checked — concern; but where treifah bubbles not common, although bubbles common — permit when lost, all the more if did not check after bubbles — for even in healthy lung we hold no examination needed, as explained beginning siman 37; and perhaps this is Taz's intent: we do not rule thus that bubble only in lung — meaning since lung lenient, no examination needed, for healthy lung itself we hold no examination; and see Mishbetzot: if worms stuck inside thin intestines part and opposite outside boil or wart — treifah — see; and my books s.k. 37 what I wrote on this.`,
  },
};
